"text": "Entries 1–8 run in /implement's first pass; 9–12 are the live round, each an operator entry fired on its own word (`inputs#I1` item 3); 13–20 run after the round. No `smoke` entry: the chunk touches no boot path (it changes no product source before the round), and a `round`/`live` leg is not used — every live start is an `operator` entry by the directive. No mutation run (`inputs#I1` item 6). No guard test is added, so no `gate.py control` is owed. The rigs are instruments this chunk builds, and entries 2, 4–7 and 13 assert their own closing lines (step 3's `{subcommand}: green|red|unread — …`, step 5's `words: 4 forks · …`). Their must-pass and must-fail readings are /implement's one-shot run before entry 2's first graded use, the readings written to `evidence/rehearsal.md`: `live-read.py words` over the four-line file reads `words: 4 forks · 0 answered` and over a three-line copy exits 3; `live-read.py start` over a planted `events.ndjson` whose line 2 is a `budget-gate` kind reads red, and over the rehearsal's own lines green; `live-read.py box` over the committed `fixtures/claude/2.1.302/Screen.ready.json` (a footer-literal row) reads `box-only: no`, and over a scratch copy cut to the box's rule and prompt rows reads `box-only: yes`; each `live-*` subcommand of `live-hold.py` and `live-tap.py` run with `founder-words.md` at `unanswered` exits 3 and starts nothing (the `rehearse*` subcommands read no founder line). Revision (step 3a): the `latest` check's must-pass and must-fail readings join that one-shot run, written to the same file — `live-read.py latest` over the real link reads `latest: 2.1.325 · cli 2.1.325 · match`, exit 0; over a scratch link in the session scratchpad pointing at `./2.1.326` it reads `refused: latest 2.1.326 is not 2.1.325`, exit 3; over an absent link `refused: latest unread`, exit 3; and with the founder lines answered, each `live-*` subcommand run while its `latest` read is refused exits 3 and makes and starts nothing — rehearsed through the function's link argument, never by moving the real link (`mise` is not touched). Entries 1–8 ran green twice in run `2026-11-15T07-52-35-implement`; the rigs change at step 3a, so /implement re-fires them. Entries 9–12 are first fired only after those readings stand. A red entry 9 (a 2.1.325 verify that does not stamp `0 fail`) leaves v2-02 and v2-03 unread: entries 10 and 11 then refuse (`refused: no green live-verify for this cli`), start nothing, and the round records both caps `unread`. Revision 2 (step 3b): entry 9 was fired once and stands red as fired (`9 e2e red · exit 0 ✗ (exit 1)`, run `2026-11-15T08-33-57-implement`): its stamp read `0 fail`, its recording was refused. Entry 21 — no start, no write — reads that kept stamp, and v2-02 and v2-03 rest on it. `stamp-check` is an instrument this chunk builds: its readings join the one-shot run before entry 21's first graded use, written to `evidence/rehearsal.md` — over the kept home `stamp-check: pass — stamped 2.1.325 · 21 of 21 relied pass · stamps sha256 86f763d8f43f5281 · …`, exit 0 (must-pass); over a scratch copy of that home in the session scratchpad with one relied row set `fail` `stamp-check: red — relied row {id} reads fail`, exit 1 (must-fail, `rehearse-derive`'s fail arm's case); over an absent base `stamp-check: unread — kept home absent`, exit 1 (must-fail). Entry 21 is listed last so no entry number moves; it reads a tmpfs home, so after a reboot it reads `unread` (red) and v2-02 and v2-03 are unread. Revision 4 (steps 3c, 3d; inputs#I21, inputs#I22): entry 10 was fired once and stands red as fired (`10 e2e red · exit 0 ✗ (exit 1)`, run `2026-11-15T09-11-10-implement`, no `session-start`). Entry 12 no longer runs the MCP tap: it is entry 10's re-fire (`live-hold.py live-wrapped`, start 9), its own reading, fired after entry 11 and only behind step 3d's gate; the tap's `live` is fired by no entry. `screens-check` is an instrument this chunk builds (entry 22, listed last so no entry number moves): its readings join the one-shot run before entry 22's first graded use, written to `evidence/rehearsal.md` — over the rehearsal's own summary `screens-check: clean — …`, exit 0 (must-pass); over a scratch copy in the session scratchpad with one line's `found` set to a row of screen text `screens-check: red — …`, exit 1 (must-fail); over a planted `screens-*.json` in a scratch copy of the work dir's layout `screens-check: red — …`, exit 1 (must-fail). The rehearsal (entries 5 and 6) reads its frames present under `target/live-round-screens/` and its summary clean. Entry 18 (hygiene) is fired with the frames in place. Revision 5 (step 3e; inputs#I26): entry 11's first firing stands red as fired (`11 e2e red · exit 0 ✗ (exit 1) · 0.11s`, run `2026-11-15T09-38-40-implement`, no start spent). Its second firing is the same entry 11, named as such in the evidence; entry 12 is unchanged. `rehearse-reuse` is a rehearsal like entries 4–7 (entry 23, listed last so no entry number moves), green before entry 11's second firing; its closing line is read whole into `evidence/rehearsal.md`. Revision 6 (step 3f; inputs#I29): entry 11's second firing stands red as fired (`11 e2e red · exit 0 ✗ (exit 1) · 40.15s`, run `2026-11-15T09-49-21-implement`, start 8 spent; second firing, the first raised before any start (FileExistsError, make_cwd)). Entry 24 `rehearse-trust-form` is a rehearsal like entries 4–7, green before entry 12's word. Entry 25 `status-row` is an instrument this chunk builds: its readings join a one-shot run before its first graded use, written to `evidence/rehearsal.md` — over a scratch frame file in the session scratchpad carrying a box rule with a row below it `status-row: present`, exit 0 (must-pass); over one with nothing below the lower rule `status-row: absent`, exit 1 (must-fail); over an absent file `status-row: unread — frame absent`, exit 1 (must-fail). The re-derived `selected` is read the same way: over a scratch frame with two unnumbered option rows, the `❯` on the second, index 1."
}
